\n\n3. **Construction of admissible configurations.**  \n\n   *For any \\(n\\ge 3\\):*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny, so the configuration uses \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *For \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=1\\):*  \n   Use the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;x+y=3,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are diagonal (non\u2011sunny); \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n   The six points of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le4\\}\\) are covered:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\in L_1,\\\\\n   &(1,2),(2,1)\\in L_2,\\\\\n   &(1,1)\\in L_3 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n=3\\):*  \n   If only one non\u2011sunny line were used, the remaining two lines would be sunny. Any sunny line can contain at most one of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) (any line through two of them is horizontal, vertical or diagonal, i.e. non\u2011sunny). Thus at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting \\(k\\ge 2\\). Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that for \\(n\\ge4\\) at least \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, so the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(k_{\\max}=n-n=0\\).  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the minimal number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(2\\); consequently \\(k\\) can be \\(0\\) (all three lines horizontal) or \\(1\\) (the construction above). No larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a **non\u2011sunny** line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=b\\;(b\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad x=a\\;(a\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad x+y=c\\;(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Minimal number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of distinct lines satisfy the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{b\\mid y=b\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{a\\mid x=a\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\;v=|\\mathcal V|,\\;d=|\\mathcal D|,\\qquad\nm=h+v+d\\;(= \\text{number of non\u2011sunny lines}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is covered iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\mathcal V\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in\\mathcal H\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in\\mathcal D .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) possible diagonal sums,\n\\[\nS=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal D|=d\\), at least \\(n-d\\) elements of \\(S\\) are **missing** from \\(\\mathcal D\\).\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\max\\bigl(S\\setminus\\mathcal D\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the largest missing sum.  \nBecause the complement of a set of size \\(d\\) in a set of size \\(n\\) has size \\(n-d\\), the largest missing element satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge n+1-d .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(s\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_s=\\{(i,s-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\le n+1\\), every point of \\(P_s\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) is not present (by definition of \\(s\\)), therefore each point of \\(P_s\\) must be covered by a horizontal or a vertical line.  \nTwo distinct points of \\(P_s\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates; consequently a single horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(P_s\\), and the same holds for a vertical line. Hence we need at least \\(|P_s|=s-1\\) distinct horizontals/verticals, i.e.\n\n\\[\ns-1\\le h+v = m-d .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-d \\le m-d \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad m\\ge n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** covering of \\(T_n\\) by non\u2011sunny lines uses at least \\(n\\) such lines whenever \\(n\\ge 4\\) (for \\(n=3\\) the inequality only forces \\(m\\ge 2\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of configurations\n\n#### 2.1  All lines non\u2011sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non\u2011sunny and satisfy (6): a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x+y=3,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are diagonal (non\u2011sunny); \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny.  \nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)&\\in L_1,\\\\\n(1,2),(2,1)&\\in L_2,\\\\\n(1,1)&\\in L_3 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\nIf at most one non\u2011sunny line were used, the remaining two lines would be sunny.  \nAny line passing through two distinct points of the set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) is either horizontal, vertical or diagonal, i.e. non\u2011sunny. Consequently each sunny line can contain **at most one** of these three points, so at least three sunny lines would be required \u2013 a contradiction. Hence \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the minimal number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(2\\) (Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(m\\ge2\\)