line also meets a given diagonal in at most one point.  \nHence, when there are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of the missing diagonal that can be covered is at most \\(k\\).\n\nTherefore for every missing sum we must have  \n\n\\[\nc-1\\le k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWith \\(k\\ge2\\) this forces \\(c\\le k+1\\le3\\).  \nThus the only possible missing sums are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\); consequently at most two diagonals are absent and  \n\n\\[\nd\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n=m+k\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\), (8) yields  \n\n\\[\nh=v=0,\\qquad d=n-2,\\qquad k=2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence the missing diagonals are exactly \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).  \nTheir point sets are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line intersecting the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the two points \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), leaving at least one uncovered – contradicting (4).\n\nThus the assumption \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible, and we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### 2.1  All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nAll lines are of the form (3) with slope \\(0\\); therefore they are non‑sunny and \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\n(which are non‑sunny) and add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_c\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\) whose sum equals \\(c\\ge3\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any \\(D_c\\) is \\((1,1)\\) (its sum is \\(2\\)), and \\((1,1)\\) belongs to \\(S\\).  \nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\nBoth constructions use \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nFrom (10) we have \\(k\\le1\\); from the constructions of §2 we have \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) both realizable.  \nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nis the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
