            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly 0 or exactly 1 of the \\(n\\) lines may be sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Re‑formulation.**  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal \\((y=b)\\), vertical \\((x=a)\\) or diagonal \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{b\\mid y=b\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal V=\\{a\\mid x=a\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)\\text{ is covered }\\Longleftrightarrow a\\in\\mathcal V\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in\\mathcal H\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in\\mathcal D .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose at least two sunny lines are present.  \n   Let \\(d=|\\mathcal D|\\) be the number of diagonal (non‑sunny) lines.  \n   The set of possible diagonal sums is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |S|=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Because only the \\(d\\) sums in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are realised, at least \\(n-d\\) sums are **missing**.  \n   For a missing sum \\(c\\) the points \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-