1,1)\\) (there are \\(c-1\\) of them) must be covered by the remaining lines.  \n   Any sunny line intersects the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in **exactly one** point (its slope is not \\(-1\\)), and a horizontal or vertical line can also meet a given diagonal in at most one point.  \n   Hence **each missing diagonal can contribute at most two covered points** when there are exactly two sunny lines.  \n   Consequently every missing diagonal must satisfy \\(c-1\\le 2\\), i.e. \\(c\\le 3\\).  \n   Therefore the only possible missing sums are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\); at most two diagonals can be absent, so \\(d\\ge n-2\\).\n\n   If two sunny lines are used, the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   n = (\\text{non‑sunny lines}) + (\\text{sunny lines})\n       = (|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+d) + 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(d\\ge n-2\\), we must have \\(|\\mathcal H|=|\\mathcal V|=0\\) and \\(d=n-2\\).  \n   The missing diagonals are then exactly \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).  \n   Their point sets are \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Two sunny lines can cover at most **one** point of the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) (any line meeting that diagonal has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line).  \n   Hence the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by only two sunny lines – a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore **no configuration with two or more sunny lines exists**; i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \n   All lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   (these are non‑sunny) and add one sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\) with a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The diagonal lines cover every point whose sum is \\(c\\ge 3\\); the only uncovered point is \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(S\\).  \n   Hence all required points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (2) we have \\(k\\le 1\\); the constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a **non‑sunny** line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=b\\;(b\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad x=a\\;(a\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad x+y=c\\;(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\; \\exists L\\in\\mathcal L\\text{ with }(a,b)\\in L .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{b\\mid y=b\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal V=\\{a\\mid x=a\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal D=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\;v=|\\mathcal V|,\\;d=|\\mathcal D|,\\qquad\nm=h+v+d\\;(=\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is covered iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\mathcal V\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in\\mathcal H\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in\\mathcal D .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At most one sunny line\n\nAssume that at least two sunny lines are present; denote their number by \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nThe total number of lines is \\(n=m+k\\